Subject: A stage compiled for a known buffer is filed under the bytes its compilation read

The plug-in compiles CoreAnimation's generic uber shader again for the 52 bytes
of its FragmentShaderConfig (compileStage's KnownBuffer), and the stage was
cached under all 52. The structure's last field ends at byte 49; the three
after it, and the padding inside it, are whatever the buffer held before.
The same configuration came with other garbage, was another key, and was
compiled again: 0.45 to 1.1 s in the background each time, the compiler taken
meanwhile. The phone's cache held 1313 stages, 377 of them one stage of 3124
bytes; a frame trace holds 25 contents for 19 configurations.

The folding of the known buffer's reads (foldKnownLoad) now records which
bytes it read. A stage depends on those and on no others: a buffer that holds
the same values there compiles to the same stage, because what is read next is
decided by what was read before. Beside its stages each function keeps an index
(<hash>.known: the mask of bytes read, their values, the stage's file), and a
known buffer that matches a record is that stage, read from the cache.
CompiledStage::knownReads carries the mask (hakios_metal_program_known_reads);
a stage filed under all its bytes by an earlier build stands for exactly them.

CompileTimes also says how long the front end and the folding took.

tools/stagecc compiles one function of a .metallib as the driver does, on a
host with the compiler (a native build of Mesa's archives with the AIR front
end): what it comes to, the bytes of a known buffer it read, and the time of
each part. With CoreAnimation's generic variant from the phone's pipeline
library (outside this tree): 32064 instruction words unspecialised, as the
phone says; for one configuration 96 words in 170 to 210 ms on this host --
about 60 the front end over the whole shader, 5 to 14 the folding, 105 to 120
the lowering and ir3 -- and the same configuration with other bytes after its
last field comes from the cache in 0.4 ms.


